WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:26.053
For seven days our ship had been tossed at sea.

2
00:00:26.054 --> 00:00:28.716
The broken masts had gone overboard.

3
00:00:28.717 --> 00:00:34.599
Leaks had sprung everywhere, and the water was rising rapidly as the raging storm grew stronger.

4
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.621
My wife, Elizabeth, gathered our four young sons around her in our cabin.

5
00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:44.814
She was encouraging them with calm and loving words.

6
00:00:44.815 --> 00:00:49.634
This was not the scene we had pictured when we set out from Switzerland to establish a new colony.

7
00:00:49.635 --> 00:00:56.815
Amid the roar of the thundering waves, I suddenly heard a seaman cry, "Land! Land!"

8
00:00:56.816 --> 00:01:02.686
At the same instant, the ship struck something with a jolt that threw everyone to the deck of the cabin.

9
00:01:02.687 --> 00:01:10.481
Above the dreadful sounds of the ship breaking up, I heard the captain shout, "Lower the lifeboats! Abandon ship!"

10
00:01:10.482 --> 00:01:15.808
"Abandon ship!" I exclaimed. The words went like a dagger through my heart.

11
00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:19.613
But seeing my children's terror, I hid my own fear.

12
00:01:19.614 --> 00:01:24.408
"Take courage, my boys!" I cried. "We're all still above water.

13
00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:28.533
There is land nearby. Let's do our best to reach it."

14
00:01:28.534 --> 00:01:30.917
With that I went up on deck.

15
00:01:30.918 --> 00:01:35.249
Imagine my horror when I saw all the lifeboats out on the water.

16
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.427
The crew had abandoned us!

17
00:01:37.428 --> 00:01:43.353
But as I looked around in despair, I realized that our situation was not hopeless.

18
00:01:43.354 --> 00:01:48.903
Although the bow of the ship had been destroyed, our cabin remained unbroken in the stern.

19
00:01:48.904 --> 00:01:52.342
I could just make out some land through the rain.

20
00:01:52.343 --> 00:01:55.225
I returned to my family and forced a smile.

21
00:01:55.226 --> 00:01:59.185
"If the wind and waves die down tomorrow, we can go ashore."

22
00:01:59.186 --> 00:02:02.490
"Then we must find food," said my wife.

23
00:02:02.491 --> 00:02:06.192
"We'll need all our strength tomorrow."

24
00:02:06.193 --> 00:02:14.925
After a good supper, the three youngest boys—Ernest, 13; Jack, 11; and Franz, 8—were soon asleep.

25
00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:20.058
But at 15, Fritz was old enough to understand the danger and to stay up with us.

26
00:02:20.059 --> 00:02:23.146
"Father, you and I can swim," he said.

27
00:02:23.147 --> 00:02:27.561
"But don't you think we should make life preservers for Mother and the boys?"

28
00:02:27.562 --> 00:02:30.092
"An excellent idea!" I told Fritz.

29
00:02:30.093 --> 00:02:34.270
"Let's make some right now in case we need to escape the ship during the night."

30
00:02:34.271 --> 00:02:38.988
We searched the ship and soon got hold of enough empty flasks and canisters.

31
00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:47.779
Connecting them together, we made life preservers, which we all quickly put on.

32
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:55.053
Fritz and his brothers now slept soundly while Elizabeth and I kept watch all night.

33
00:02:55.054 --> 00:02:58.511
The next morning we gathered on the remaining portion of the deck.

34
00:02:58.512 --> 00:03:02.498
"Where are the sailors?" asked my youngest son, Franz.

35
00:03:02.499 --> 00:03:06.281
"Have they taken away all the boats?" asked Jack.

36
00:03:06.282 --> 00:03:08.021
I nodded gravely.

37
00:03:08.022 --> 00:03:12.961
"The sea will soon be calm enough for swimming," Fritz reassured his younger brothers.

38
00:03:12.962 --> 00:03:16.445
"That's fine for you and Father!" exclaimed Ernest.

39
00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:18.976
"But think of Mother and the rest of us!

40
00:03:18.977 --> 00:03:22.497
Why not build a raft so we can all go ashore together?"

41
00:03:22.498 --> 00:03:26.587
"It would be difficult to build a raft that would carry us safely," I said.

42
00:03:26.588 --> 00:03:31.248
"But let's think about that while we explore the ship to see what might be of use to us."

43
00:03:31.249 --> 00:03:37.305
While I checked on the food and water, my wife took Franz to help her feed the animals on board.

44
00:03:37.306 --> 00:03:40.004
Jack went to the captain's cabin.

45
00:03:40.005 --> 00:03:49.911
When he opened the door, out sprang two large but friendly dogs!

46
00:03:49.912 --> 00:03:52.833
Back in our cabin we displayed our treasures.

47
00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:57.481
Fritz brought a couple of guns, powder flasks, and plenty of bullets.

48
00:03:57.482 --> 00:04:03.628
Ernest had nails, a saw, and a hammer while other tools stuck out of his pockets.

49
00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:09.742
"Look at these nice sharp hooks!" said little Franz, proudly showing us a box of fishhooks.

50
00:04:09.743 --> 00:04:14.981
"Well done, Franz!" I said. "Those may save our lives by keeping us fed."

51
00:04:14.982 --> 00:04:18.224
"I have some good news," said my dear wife.

52
00:04:18.225 --> 00:04:26.084
"Some useful animals are still alive: a cow, a donkey, two goats, six sheep, and a fine pig."

53
00:04:26.085 --> 00:04:29.689
"Now we just have to figure out how to get ashore," I said.

54
00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.191
"Can't we each get into a big tub and float there?" said Jack.

55
00:04:34.192 --> 00:04:37.261
"I've often sailed like that around the pond at home."

56
00:04:37.262 --> 00:04:39.622
"A splendid idea!" I said.

57
00:04:39.623 --> 00:04:45.568
We soon found four tall wooden casks floating in the hold.

58
00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:52.343
After dragging them on deck, we managed—with some difficulty—to saw them in half.

59
00:04:52.344 --> 00:04:57.212
I was proud of our work, but my wife looked in dismay at the eight tubs.

60
00:04:57.213 --> 00:05:00.693
"I'll never get into one of those!" she declared.

61
00:05:00.694 --> 00:05:02.801
"Just wait," I said.

62
00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:08.128
I arranged my tubs in a row, connecting them with planks along the bottom and sides.

63
00:05:08.129 --> 00:05:14.517
When I finished, I was confident our boat would be perfectly safe in smooth water.

64
00:05:14.518 --> 00:05:23.401
There was only one problem: Our boat was so heavy that we couldn't move it an inch.
